{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Lake Vostok's Cousin: Methane Seep Life Under Antarctica's Lake Untersee",
    "date": "2026-11-12",
    "lesson": "Lake Untersee is a perennially ice-covered Antarctic lake with a sharp chemocline: an oxic upper layer sits atop an anoxic, methane-rich lower layer where methanogen-derived gas seeps upward. Chemolithotrophic bacteria cluster at that boundary, oxidizing methane and sulfide for energy completely independent of sunlight filtering through 3-4m of perennial ice. This scenario places a chemolithotroph at a mid-column chemical plume while heterotrophs scavenge organic falls from the thin microbial mats above. The lesson: not all dark, cold habitats are starved -- a steady chemical seep can support a permanent, depth-locked community, but only within the narrow band where the plume actually reaches.",
    "citation": "McKay et al. 2005, Lake Untersee limnology; Andersen et al. microbial mat studies, Dry Valleys Antarctica",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Lake Untersee, East Antarctica - perennially ice-covered lake with methane seep and anoxic chemocline"
  },
